# Forge Server Launcher

Desktop launcher prototype for a Minecraft Java / Forge server.

Run:

```bat
ForgeServerLauncher.exe
```

Backup run option:

```bat
start-launcher.bat
```

No .NET wrapper option:

```bat
start-direct.bat
```

What it does now:

- opens a real Windows desktop window through WPF, not a web page;
- scans installed Minecraft versions in `%APPDATA%\.minecraft\versions`;
- uses bundled Java 17 from `runtime\java17` first;
- can fall back to TLauncher Java 17, `JAVA_HOME`, common install paths, or PATH;
- starts the selected installed Minecraft/Forge client version;
- uses an offline profile, useful for local/offline-mode servers;
- passes `--server` and `--port` so the client connects to your server.

Packaging:

- `ForgeServerLauncher.exe` is a small Windows starter for `Launcher.ps1`;
- `start-direct.bat` runs `Launcher.ps1` directly through Windows PowerShell;
- `runtime\java17` is the bundled full Java 17 runtime/JDK used by Forge;
- `dist\ForgeServerLauncher` is the portable folder;
- `dist\ForgeServerLauncher.zip` is the packed copy.

Rebuild exe:

```powershell
powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "..\ForgeServerLauncherBuild\build-exe.ps1"
```

Important:

- the current exe is framework-dependent and uses the installed .NET runtime;
- a self-contained exe can be built later when NuGet access is available;
- Java is bundled, so TLauncher or a system Java install is not required after packaging;
- this does not download Minecraft, Forge, assets, or mods yet;
- install the needed client version first, for example `Forge 1.20.1`;
- your server currently listens on `127.0.0.1:25565` by default.

